 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Neural Volume Scattering Prediction](8439-neural-volume-scattering-prediction.md)
+A method for rendering participating media that uses a neural network to predict the scattering distribution at the centers of sampling templates to solve the radiative transfer equation. It sets itself apart from its siblings by decomposing input features into density, transmission, and phase, and by using specific sampling templates (diffuse and highlight) to simplify the network structure and input parameters. Choose it when shadow handling and reduced sampling requirements are needed, as it addresses RPNN's inability to handle shadows and its requirement for large sampling.
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** The method is reported to be approximately a thousand times faster than path tracing while producing nearly identical results [S1].
-*   **Accuracy:** Rendering results are described as "almost identical" to path tracing [S1].
-*   **Complexity:** The complexity depends on the MLP inference time and the Monte Carlo sampling for direct/single scattering. The hierarchical stencil construction adds preprocessing cost [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** The method is specifically designed for high-order scattering media like atmospheric clouds [S1]. The reliance on a pre-trained network means it may not generalize well to unseen cloud structures without retraining [G].
